  "worked_solution": "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{16})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/7a630b4582049bf2a2144ce484069a5301e7363a35da38e7d86ff2fee22543d4.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4.5$ units up. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=4.5,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,5.5).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{16})}^{x}+4.5$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/35c5c0ef4850371b35fa43b9f2944e98919199bbacf41453973167522cf04f35.png)",
